From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 09:32:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 144/185] Settle to pause before the retry, and what Doom
 actually does

Two things, one shipped and one written down.

Shipped: cd_read_block's second fill was starting against a drive still
in flight. cd_fetch's failure path tells the drive to pause and returns
immediately, so the retry faced exactly the coin toss the per-attempt
reissue exists to avoid, one level up. Sega's hardware notes are
explicit that a read issued from pause starts instantly -- in state 4
the head keeps reading and jumps back a track each pass, hovering over
the right place rather than travelling. Wait for state 4, bounded by
twenty of the drive's own exchanges and by the fill's clock.

The console said this in the one way that could not be misread: both
reads that failed at the desktop -- opening D:, then opening the file
-- raised GEMDOS's critical-error alert, and both succeeded on the
first press of Retry. The difference between our retry and the human's
is a second of the drive settling.

Written down, from Doom CD32X Fusion's sub program in the cartridge at
0xC40 (its disc is not bootable either -- sector 0 is empty -- so it is
a Mode 1 data reader, the same problem we have):

  6866: movem.l d0-d1/a0-a1,-(sp)   d0 start, d1 count, a0 dest
        move.w #$89,d0   CDCSTOP
        jsr $5F22
        movea.l sp,a0
        move.w #$20,d0   ROMREADN
        jsr $5F22

One CDCSTOP and one ROMREADN for a run of COUNT sectors, then
CDCSTAT/CDCREAD/CDCTRN/CDCACK per sector with no drive command in
between. This driver sends a Play per 2048-byte sector and re-decides
the seek each time: 69 seeks for 84 sectors on the console.

And its init, which is four instructions we do not have:

  65fe: lea $694C,a0
        move.w #$10,d0   DRV_INIT
        jsr $5F22
        move.w #0,d1
        move.w #$96,d0   CDCSETMODE
        jsr $5F22

DRV_INIT appears at three sites in Doom. The claim in this file that
"no Sega code that reads a sector calls DRV_INIT" is wrong, and the
console had already contradicted it: DRV_INIT is what brings this drive
out of its no-information state. CDCSETMODE we have never called at
all -- we write a hard-coded CTRL0/CTRL1 ladder and let a heuristic
pick a rung, against 95% chaff out of the decoder.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 1cca856..3c328ed 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -3043,6 +3043,39 @@ static BOOL cd_read_block(ULONG block, UBYTE *buf)
                 cd_leash = go ? (UWORD)(leash * 2) : leash;
                 if (cd_fill(lba))
                     break;
+
+                /* Let it reach the pause before trying again.
+                 *
+                 * cd_fetch's failure path tells the drive to pause and
+                 * returns immediately, so the retry above was starting
+                 * against a drive still in flight -- the coin toss the
+                 * per-attempt reissue was written to avoid, repeated
+                 * one level up. Sega's hardware notes are explicit
+                 * that a read issued from pause starts instantly,
+                 * because in state 4 the head keeps reading and jumps
+                 * back a track each pass; it is hovering over the
+                 * right place rather than travelling.
+                 *
+                 * The console said this out loud, in the one way this
+                 * project could not misread. Both reads that failed at
+                 * the desktop -- opening D:, then opening the file --
+                 * came back with GEMDOS's critical-error alert, and
+                 * both succeeded on the first press of Retry. The
+                 * difference between our retry and the human's is a
+                 * second or two of the drive settling, and that is all
+                 * this is.
+                 *
+                 * Bounded twice, by the drive's own exchanges and by
+                 * the fill's clock, so a drive that never pauses costs
+                 * a quarter of a second and not the session. */
+                {
+                    UBYTE base = cdd_irqs;
+
+                    while ((UBYTE)(cdd_irqs - base) < 20 && cd_time_left()) {
+                        if (cdd_valid && cdd_stat[0] == 0x4)
+                            break;
+                    }
+                }
                 if (go) {
                     cd_leash = leash;
                     return FALSE;
